# Service Operations console configuration.
#
# The console keeps no account data in this repository. Copy the ids of the
# account you point it at into these variables and export them before starting
# the server, for example:
#
#   export $(grep -v '^#' .env | xargs)
#   npm run dev
#
# On Node 20.6 or newer the file can be loaded directly:
#
#   node --env-file=.env server.mjs
#
# Every value below is a placeholder. Replace it with an id from your own
# account; never commit a real value.

# Brand the console is scoped to (Zendesk brand id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_BRAND_ID=12345678901231

# Ticket field that carries the request type (ticket field id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_REQUEST_TYPE_FIELD_ID=12345678901232

# Ticket field that carries the system (ticket field id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_SYSTEM_FIELD_ID=12345678901233

# View id for the open queue (Zendesk view id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_VIEW_OPEN_ID=12345678901234

# View id for the untriaged queue (Zendesk view id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_VIEW_UNTRIAGED_ID=12345678901235

# View id for the aging report (Zendesk view id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_VIEW_AGING_ID=12345678901236

# View id for the request-type report (Zendesk view id).
ZENDESK_CONSOLE_VIEW_REQUEST_TYPE_ID=12345678901237

# The account comes from the OAuth client file
# (~/.config/codex-zendesk/client.json), which names it as a "baseUrl" or a
# "subdomain". The console does not follow ZENDESK_BASE_URL or
# ZENDESK_SUBDOMAIN: those name an account for the connector, and the console
# would send the client secret there.
#
# That path is the default. Set ZENDESK_OAUTH_CLIENT_FILE when the file lives
# elsewhere, and write the whole path: a leading "~" is not expanded.
#
# The file holds the client secret, and the console posts that secret to the
# account the file names, so it has to be one only its owner can read: the
# console refuses to use the file when it is accessible to its group or by
# others, and its dashboard then answers 503 with this message:
# "The Zendesk OAuth client file must be readable only by its owner (chmod 600)."
# The console keeps running in that case, and the secret is never read into
# memory and never posted to the account. The check is skipped on Windows,
# where Node reports synthetic permission bits regardless of the file's ACLs.
#
# ZENDESK_OAUTH_CLIENT_FILE=/home/example-user/.config/codex-zendesk/client.json
#
# The console asks for read scopes only, and it asks for the scope it needs:
# it reads two ticket views, and Zendesk scopes a view by its own scope.
# `ticket_views:read` is documented as "view ticket views and the tickets
# they return"; `tickets:read` covers the ticket fields the dashboard reads
# off the rows a view returns.
#
# Both are required. A scope that cannot read a view, or the ticket fields on
# it, is a configuration error rather than a dashboard that quietly shows
# every ticket as "Unclassified", and the console names what is missing. The
# broad "read" scope covers both of them, because it covers every GET
# endpoint, so a client that grants it needs nothing else: set the scope to
# "read" and the console asks for that.
#
# The scope comes from ZENDESK_CONSOLE_OAUTH_SCOPE, and then from
# ZENDESK_OAUTH_SCOPE. The scope recorded in the client file is the
# connector's scope and is not used here: it carries write scopes, and the
# console writes nothing. Write scopes are dropped from whatever is
# configured, for the same reason.
#
# The scope has to be allowed on the Zendesk OAuth client as well. A client
# that is not granted it answers the token request with "invalid_scope", and
# the console reports that.
#
# ZENDESK_CONSOLE_OAUTH_SCOPE=ticket_views:read tickets:read

# The Host headers the console answers to besides the loopback names. A
# reverse proxy in front of the console forwards the name the browser asked
# for, not the loopback address, so a console behind a proxy needs the
# proxy's name here — otherwise every request is refused with 403.
#
# The value is matched against the whole Host header, port included, so a
# proxy that forwards the port has to list it. The list is empty by default,
# and the console answers only the loopback names.
#
# The console binds to the loopback interface, so only a proxy on this machine
# can reach it. The proxy's own access control is what stands in front of
# the console then; see docs/operations.md.
#
# ZENDESK_CONSOLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=console.example.com
